1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:05,266
When light bounces off a surface, it's reflected, a bit like a ball bouncing off a wall.

2
00:00:05,266 --> 00:00:10,888
The ray hitting the mirror is the incident ray, and the ray bouncing off is the reflected ray.

3
00:00:10,888 --> 00:00:14,557
The normal is an imaginary line at right angles to the mirror.

4
00:00:15,616 --> 00:00:20,468
The law of reflection says the angle of incidence equals the angle of reflection,

5
00:00:20,468 --> 00:00:26,859
both measured from the normal. The incident ray, the reflected ray and the normal all lie in the same plane.

6
00:00:27,918 --> 00:00:32,888
So if a ray hits a mirror at 35 degrees to the normal, it's reflected at 35 degrees.

7
00:00:33,948 --> 00:00:39,392
Smooth, shiny surfaces like mirrors give specular reflection: all rays bounce the same way,

8
00:00:39,392 --> 00:00:40,693
making a sharp image.

9
00:00:40,693 --> 00:00:46,315
Rough surfaces like paper scatter the rays in different directions: this is diffuse reflection.

10
00:00:47,374 --> 00:00:53,173
When you look in a flat, or plane, mirror, light from you reflects off the mirror into your eyes.

11
00:00:53,173 --> 00:00:58,795
Your brain traces the rays back in straight lines, so the image seems to be behind the mirror,

12
00:00:58,795 --> 00:01:01,043
even though no light comes from there.

13
00:01:02,102 --> 00:01:08,020
So the image is virtual: it can't be caught on a screen. It's upright, the same size as the object,

14
00:01:08,020 --> 00:01:11,984
and the same distance behind the mirror as the object is in front.

15
00:01:11,984 --> 00:01:16,896
And it's laterally inverted: raise your right hand, and your image raises its left!

16
00:01:17,955 --> 00:01:24,109
A student shines a ray box at a plane mirror and measures both angles from the normal with a protractor.

17
00:01:25,168 --> 00:01:28,423
At 10 degrees the reflected ray is also at 10 degrees.

18
00:01:28,423 --> 00:01:32,269
At 20 degrees she measures 21, and at 30 degrees she measures 29.

19
00:01:33,328 --> 00:01:39,009
The points lie very close to a straight line through the origin, where the two angles are equal.

20
00:01:40,068 --> 00:01:46,281
Pause and decide: do her results support the law of reflection, and why are some readings one degree out?
